    "reading": {
        "body": "Arizona's tracked 2026 legislative session produced 30 bills, and unlike many states in this dataset, most directly address artificial intelligence rather than touching it incidentally: state agency AI rules, AI service disclosure requirements, AI curriculum in public schools, AI use in nursing tasks, chatbot regulation, AI generated content verification, and AI used in privileged communications and divorce arbitration all appear as separate bills.\n\nThat density and specificity, distinct bills for AI in schools, AI in healthcare, AI in family law, and AI in government procurement, rather than one omnibus AI bill, suggests Arizona's legislature is treating AI regulation as a set of sector specific problems to be addressed individually rather than through a single comprehensive framework.\n\nA smaller cluster of bills addresses AI adjacent harms to minors and consumers: non-consensual synthetic sexual depictions, social media age verification, and rental algorithmic pricing, connecting Arizona's AI legislation to broader consumer and child protection concerns rather than AI regulation in isolation.\n\nThe data does not indicate which of these 30 bills passed, failed, or remain pending, nor does it include bill text beyond title and number, so the current legislative status of Arizona's AI framework is not established by this record.",
